\ The 46 visible drops, before any pouring into the six bowls.
\ This implements section G of the canonical main-branch rules.
\ At visible drop i, the old drop history gives i-1, i-3, and i-7.
\ The first seven old values are the hidden drops, in reverse order.
\ All calculations use exact wide quantities; no CAL number can overflow.
\ CAL compilation and execution have not yet been verified.

A drop history has
  a wide quantity called first previous,
  a wide quantity called second previous,
  a wide quantity called third previous,
  a wide quantity called fourth previous,
  a wide quantity called fifth previous,
  a wide quantity called sixth previous,
  a wide quantity called seventh previous.

A dough ingredient has
  a wide quantity called stone,
  a wide quantity called count,
  a wide quantity called contribution.

A visible calculation has
  a number called index,
  a number called grinding step,
  a number called value factor,
  a number called first previous factor,
  a number called third previous factor,
  a number called seventh previous factor,
  a wide quantity called value,
  a wide quantity called grinding stone.

The previous drops are a drop history.
The dough work is a dough ingredient.
The visible work is a visible calculation.
The last visible drop is a wide quantity.

\ In the first visible drop, position 0 is hidden drop 1.
\ Position -2 is hidden drop 3, and position -6 is hidden drop 7.
To prepare the previous drops:
  Put the hidden drops' first drop into the previous drops' first previous.
  Put the hidden drops' second drop into the previous drops' second previous.
  Put the hidden drops' third drop into the previous drops' third previous.
  Put the hidden drops' fourth drop into the previous drops' fourth previous.
  Put the hidden drops' fifth drop into the previous drops' fifth previous.
  Put the hidden drops' sixth drop into the previous drops' sixth previous.
  Put the hidden drops' seventh drop into the previous drops' seventh previous.

\ Multiply one stone by its corresponding day count and add the result.
To add the dough ingredient:
  Put the dough work's stone into the dough work's contribution.
  Multiply the dough work's count by the dough work's contribution.
  Add the dough work's contribution to the visible work's value.

\ The first dough is SAVE(wheat*c + barley*t + salt*distance +
\ bitter*sum + red*direction + drop[i-1] + 3*drop[i-3] +
\ 5*drop[i-7] + i). The same definition is used for all 46 drops.
To make the visible dough:
  Put "0" into the visible work's value.
  Put the current stones' wheat into the dough work's stone.
  Put the sauce input's calculation count into the dough work's count.
  Add the dough ingredient.
  Put the current stones' barley into the dough work's stone.
  Put the sauce input's requested count into the dough work's count.
  Add the dough ingredient.
  Put the current stones' salt into the dough work's stone.
  Put the sauce input's distance count into the dough work's count.
  Add the dough ingredient.
  Put the current stones' bitter into the dough work's stone.
  Put the sauce input's sum count into the dough work's count.
  Add the dough ingredient.
  Put the current stones' red into the dough work's stone.
  Put the sauce input's direction count into the dough work's count.
  Add the dough ingredient.
  Add the previous drops' first previous to the visible work's value.
  Put the previous drops' third previous into a third term wide quantity.
  Multiply the third term wide quantity by 3.
  Add the third term wide quantity to the visible work's value.
  Put the previous drops' seventh previous into a seventh term wide quantity.
  Multiply the seventh term wide quantity by 5.
  Add the seventh term wide quantity to the visible work's value.
  Add the visible work's index to the visible work's value.
  Keep the visible work's value for the sauce.

\ Each row below gives four multipliers followed by a fixed stone.
\ A step always uses all three previous-drop positions from the same
\ drop history. These positions do not change until grinding ends.
To choose the visible grinding recipe:
  \ Grinding step 1: [3, 5, 7, 11, wheat].
  If the visible work's grinding step is 1, put 3 into the visible work's value factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 1, put 5 into the visible work's first previous factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 1, put 7 into the visible work's third previous factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 1, put 11 into the visible work's seventh previous factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 1, put the current stones' wheat into the visible work's grinding stone.
  \ Grinding step 2: [5, 7, 11, 13, barley].
  If the visible work's grinding step is 2, put 5 into the visible work's value factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 2, put 7 into the visible work's first previous factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 2, put 11 into the visible work's third previous factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 2, put 13 into the visible work's seventh previous factor.
  If the visible work's grinding step is 2, put the current stones' barley into the visible work's grinding stone.
  \ Grinding step 3: [7, 11, 13, 17, salt].
